00:35:13,216 --> 00:35:20,164
there's been this obvious explosion in first in coding and software engineering capabilities, and then with a slight lead time in math capabilities.

376
00:35:20,576 --> 00:35:25,136
That has not been associated with a corresponding increase in science and engineering capabilities.

377
00:35:25,296 --> 00:35:31,364
There has been some increase, but it's nowhere near as stratospheric as what we've seen in coding and math. Okay, why is that?

378
00:35:31,536 --> 00:35:37,124
Well, you know, there's a standard answer, which I think is basically right, which is that you know, we're at the stage of AI development right now

379
00:35:37,296 --> 00:35:42,308
where the majority of the improvements are not of capabilities improvements, are not coming from improvements in the models.

380
00:35:42,542 --> 00:35:44,228
They're coming from improvements in post-training.

381
00:35:44,494 --> 00:35:50,948
They're coming from improvements in how good can we make the reinforcement learning environments, and therefore by extension, how good are the verifiers?

382
00:35:51,502 --> 00:35:52,656
And so, because

383
00:35:53,550 --> 00:35:54,256
all modern

384
00:35:55,246 --> 00:36:03,300
AI models are trained largely or are post-trained largely through not reinforcement learning with human feedback, but reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards.

385
00:36:03,886 --> 00:36:09,860
And so if you're in a verifiable domain, then you know you can you can effectively you know you can self-improve in a completely unsupervised way.

386
00:36:10,336 --> 00:36:16,656
And so because we had formal verification tools for determining software correctness, which were things like compilers,

387
00:36:16,816 --> 00:36:24,336
type checkers, static analyzers, et cetera, and we had formal verification tools for determining mathematical correctness, which were things like proof assistants, automated theorem provers,

388
00:36:24,496 --> 00:36:28,164
interactive theorem improvers, you know, tools like lean, agda, rock, et cetera.

389
00:36:28,654 --> 00:36:39,296
Uh so as a consequence, as I think a very direct consequence, we've seen this great takeoff in capability in computation and math. What is the analog verification pipeline for physics,

390
00:36:39,598 --> 00:36:41,924
chemistry, biology, engineering, et cetera?

391
00:36:42,126 --> 00:36:43,616
Right now there kind of isn't one, right?

392
00:36:43,790 --> 00:36:45,732
And the history of this is extremely interesting.

393
00:36:46,096 --> 00:36:50,576
So if you go back to the start of the 20th century, David Hilbert posed these,

394
00:36:51,456 --> 00:36:57,284
famous 20 problems for kind of the foundations of mathematics that also strayed into physics and some other areas.

395
00:36:57,582 --> 00:37:02,256
And one of them was this famous Entscheidungs problem that, you know, this decision problem for determining,

396
00:37:02,736 --> 00:37:07,456
effectively defining a mechanical procedure for determining whether a mathematical proposition was true.

397
00:37:07,662 --> 00:37:16,736
And the attempt to solve that decision problem is what led people like uh, you know, Girdle, Turing, Church, et cetera, to develop what we now call the modern theory of computation.

398
00:37:17,134 --> 00:37:26,336
So, as a direct outcome of the Hilbert program, we developed in the early 20th century a reasoning language that let us formalize the process of mathematical reasoning,

399
00:37:26,510 --> 00:37:34,736
right? So the idea is uh, you know, it with a Turing machine, you know, you have an input, you do some transitions on that input, you produce an output.

400
00:37:34,816 --> 00:37:41,216
And that's effectively that's intended to be an ultimate, like desiccated abstraction of the process of mathematical reasoning,

401
00:37:41,376 --> 00:37:44,772
that you take in some axioms, you apply some inference rules, you produce a theorem.

402
00:37:45,326 --> 00:37:51,876
And that's turned out to be extremely fruitful, of course, right? You know, the development of the theory of computation has been one of the most fruitful kind of intellectual ideas in our history.

403
00:37:52,622 --> 00:37:58,944
Hilbert, interestingly, also had a corresponding problem to do the effectively the same thing for physics, right? To develop a uh

404
00:37:59,744 --> 00:38:09,904
to construct axiom formal axiomatic foundations for physics, or to put it in a sort of more uh uh uh you know computational way, to effectively mechanize the process of physical reasoning.

405
00:38:09,984 --> 00:38:12,964
And we could exit, we could generalize that to mechanize the process of scientific reasoning.

406
00:38:13,710 --> 00:38:16,772
But unlike the Entscheidungs problem, that problem has not been solved.

407
00:38:17,104 --> 00:38:21,700
We do not have a corresponding formal language that mechanizes the process of scientific reasoning.

408
00:38:22,144 --> 00:38:30,704
And I think, personally, and this is one of the key convictions of our company, that until such a language exists, we will not see the analog, uh, you know, the the the

409
00:38:30,944 --> 00:38:39,492
analogous capabilities developments that we've seen in math and coding in broader natural science and in engineering and broadly in anything that kind of interfaces with the physical world.

410
00:38:40,144 --> 00:38:46,020
And I think this is strictly a harder problem than the problem of kind of mechanizing mathematical reasoning.

411
00:38:46,864 --> 00:38:47,824
it's because

412
00:38:49,024 --> 00:38:51,236
it's harder in a very simple sense, which is that

413
00:38:52,110 --> 00:38:58,224
the mechanization of mathematical reasoning is a necessary but not sufficient condition for mechanizing physical reasoning, right?

414
00:38:58,384 --> 00:39:01,924
And uh we can, I'm sure we'll talk more about this in greater detail later on.

415
00:39:02,224 --> 00:39:09,476
But physical reasoning, there are at least sort of three major components of it: one of which is essentially mathematical to do with internal consistency,

416
00:39:09,664 --> 00:39:16,544
one of which is essentially theoretical to do with consistency with other presupposed physical principles. and the final of which is experimental.

417
00:39:16,784 --> 00:39:22,864
and you know, to do, uh, related to how this model actually interfaces with the physical world, or with physical reality.

418
00:39:23,118 --> 00:39:23,584
And that's

419
00:39:24,864 --> 00:39:33,124
significantly harder than the other two, and poses a completely different set of philosophical problems that I think we've not even begun really to grapple with in a serious way.

420
00:39:34,064 --> 00:39:34,544
so yes,

421
00:39:35,744 --> 00:39:41,424
the short answer to your question is I think the S and the T have really become separated. You mean the T and the M?

422
00:39:41,904 --> 00:39:50,544
Sorry, the the T and the M have become separated from the S and the E because the T and the M have you know could can effectively leverage the theory of computation and the development of

423
00:39:50,766 --> 00:39:55,524
formalized mathematical reasoning for which we have no uh no corresponding analog for S and E.

424
00:39:56,078 --> 00:40:00,144
what is it then about science that makes it so tricky to formalize compared to math?

425
00:40:00,334 --> 00:40:05,264
Is it the scientific method isn't well defined? Is it that you have to perform experiments?

426
00:40:05,454 --> 00:40:06,276
Like what is it?

427
00:40:06,894 --> 00:40:14,692
So that's definitely part of it, and uh it's an important part. But even if you even on the purely theoretical level, uh I think it's it's already more difficult.

428
00:40:14,864 --> 00:40:15,556
So actually,

429
00:40:16,430 --> 00:40:23,076
in in kind of tracking down the history of this, there is one project that came close to, I think, what we are trying to do at Lanian AI.

430
00:40:23,344 --> 00:40:27,664
And that was so Alan Turing had one official graduate student in his academic career.

431
00:40:27,918 --> 00:40:34,660
It was a guy called Robin Gandhi, uh, who was a PhD student at King's College, Cambridge under Turing in kind of late 1940s, early 1950s.

432
00:40:35,024 --> 00:40:37,664
And he wrote a thesis that is called something like

433
00:40:38,544 --> 00:40:38,784
on

434
00:40:39,824 --> 00:40:42,308
axiomatic systems in mathematics and theories in physics.

435
00:40:43,118 --> 00:40:45,732
And it's a really weird thesis. I read it several years ago.

436
00:40:45,984 --> 00:40:49,124
And the first half of it is kind of pretty boring kind of type theory stuff.

437
00:40:49,614 --> 00:40:58,464
But the second half is a really interesting but ultimately very underdeveloped research program to basically extend type theory to physics and more precisely

438
00:40:58,784 --> 00:41:04,868
to build not just a type system, but an analog of the Turing machine for physical and scientific reasoning.

439
00:41:05,104 --> 00:41:11,812
And so Gandhi was thinking, okay. if the if, if a Turing machine is an abstract machine, that that idealizes mathematical thought,

440
00:41:12,464 --> 00:41:16,304
could we have an analogous machine that basically takes in as input?

441
00:41:16,464 --> 00:41:22,500
you know, uh empirical data, sense. data, applies a process of scientific induction

442
00:41:23,278 --> 00:41:26,224
with you know, with with additional deductive principles and other things?

443
00:41:26,304 --> 00:41:33,504
You know, science is a pure induction as uh as is you know, well documented, and then produces as output effectively scientific hypotheses or physical hypotheses.

444
00:41:33,584 --> 00:41:41,584
And he effectively tried to, to the extent that I understand what he was getting at, he was basically trying to design such an abstract machine and trying to design a type system that would allow

445
00:41:41,584 --> 00:41:43,204
you to reason about such an abstract machine.

446
00:41:43,424 --> 00:41:45,824
And it didn't really work. It was an overly ambitious

447
00:41:46,704 --> 00:41:56,708
research program for kind of the second half of a PhD thesis, and he never really extended it. But there was clearly a kernel of an idea there that started to grapple with some of these issues.

448
00:41:56,944 --> 00:42:06,304
Because, yeah, so the you know, the the the key things that make it hard are yes, okay, there is math formalization that tells you is the, you know, is the model internally consistent?

449
00:42:06,478 --> 00:42:13,744
So if we take something like quantum mechanics, this would mean you know formalizing things like the theory of projective Hilbert spaces, the theory of unbounded linear operators,

450
00:42:13,824 --> 00:42:15,744
just pure like mathematical superstructure.

451
00:42:15,950 --> 00:42:21,424
No physical content there. But that you know, that's already at least as hard as formalizing mathematics, right?

452
00:42:21,504 --> 00:42:24,932
So you run into all the same issues that you do with you know proof assistance and other things.

453
00:42:25,870 --> 00:42:29,668
Then there is figuring out consistency with existing physical principles.

454
00:42:30,062 --> 00:42:35,296
So and that's really what that's effectively what distinguishes theoretical physics from mathematical physics in some sense, right?

455
00:42:35,376 --> 00:42:44,336
So this is things like uh, you know, is your is your proposed model consistent with the conservation of energy? Is it consistent with the laws of thermodynamics? Is it generally covariant?

456
00:42:44,496 --> 00:42:47,056
Is your, you know, is your time evolution unitary? These kinds of things.

457
00:42:47,296 --> 00:42:51,536
Are you saying that the theoretical physicists are concerned with the consistency, whereas the mathematical ones are not?

458
00:42:51,822 --> 00:42:52,656
Or is it vice versa?

459
00:42:52,976 --> 00:43:01,616
No, I I I I would I would think of it as, and this is maybe overly unfair on the mathematical physicists, but I would think of it as like each one is a is a superset of the

460
00:43:01,966 --> 00:43:02,736
thing that came before.

461
00:43:02,896 --> 00:43:10,576
If you're a pure math if you're purely a mathematical physicist, you might not necessarily care that your your proposed physical model is not consistent with some physical principle.

462
00:43:10,656 --> 00:43:14,820
Because you could say, well, it's just producing interesting mathematics. But what you do care about is mathematical consistency.

463
00:43:15,246 --> 00:43:19,296
If you're a theorist, you probably care about mathematical consistency, although maybe not.

464
00:43:19,376 --> 00:43:26,016
I mean, you know, like quantum field theory, famously, we don't yet know how to make that a consistent mathematical theory, but it still is extremely theoretically successful.

465
00:43:26,222 --> 00:43:28,256
So this is a slight oversimplification on my part.

466
00:43:29,102 --> 00:43:37,188
But if you're a theoretical physicist, you care not just about the internal logical consistency, but you also care about how it, you know, how it how consistent is it with other theories.

467
00:43:38,414 --> 00:43:47,136
and that's complicated because you could think that you might think that's purely mathematical deduction. That is, you know, uh you have a physical model, can I derive energy conservation?

468
00:43:47,296 --> 00:43:55,856
Can I derive entropy, you know, monotonicity, et cetera? But it's really not that simple, right? Because those principles may only be approximately true.

469
00:43:56,016 --> 00:43:59,296
They may only be idealizations that apply in specific regimes.

470
00:44:00,656 --> 00:44:05,776
there's a bunch of essentially heuristics that you have to apply at the theoretical level that you can avoid at the mathematical level.

471
00:44:05,936 --> 00:44:13,376
So you know, if you were trying to assess a theory of quantum gravity, okay, well, uh, what what desiderata do we want from a theory of quantum gravity?

472
00:44:13,456 --> 00:44:20,416
Well, at the very least, it should reproduce the Einstein equations in the limit of large mass displacements, and it should it should reproduce the Yang Mills equations

473
00:44:20,736 --> 00:44:22,496
in the limit of small mass displacements.

474
00:44:23,374 --> 00:44:25,220
But you know, would such a theory be covariant?

475
00:44:25,966 --> 00:44:26,884
Who knows, right?

476
00:44:27,086 --> 00:44:34,692
Like generally speaking, right now, we think of covariance, Lorentz covariance or general covariance, as being a required consistency condition of a physical model.

477
00:44:35,150 --> 00:44:35,456
But if

478
00:44:36,416 --> 00:44:44,016
a proposed model of quantum gravity replaces the concept of spacetime entirely and spacetime emerges as some approximation to that, to that structure, then

479
00:44:44,496 --> 00:44:51,616
you might not be able to prove general covariance formally, because it might not be true, but you may be able to prove it approximately or to derive it approximately in some regime,

480
00:44:51,696 --> 00:44:52,708
and that may be all that matters.

481
00:44:52,976 --> 00:44:59,776
So there's a kind of a mushier notion of reasoning that exists at the theoretical level that I think we have not yet correctly formalized. And that's a again.

482
00:44:59,856 --> 00:45:03,536
that's a key part of what we are initially trying to work on at Lanian AI, is trying to kind of

483
00:45:04,576 --> 00:45:12,896
take a physic, a mathematically consistent model and figure out how can we at least approximately determine its consistency with uh, you know, with other the

484
00:45:13,296 --> 00:45:19,780
other principles and theories of physics in a way that is equivalent to the way we currently assess mathematical consistency through Lean and things like that.

485
00:45:20,462 --> 00:45:21,536
And then there is the

486
00:45:22,416 --> 00:45:31,844
final level of complexity, which is which utterly dwarfs the previous two, which is, you know, how does it, you know, how like is it consistent with what we see in physical reality?

487
00:45:32,430 --> 00:45:37,604
And that's the place where, as you said, we have to we run into these issues about formalization of the scientific method.

488
00:45:37,998 --> 00:45:45,136
And in particular, we run into a bunch of limitative problems that were pointed out in the early 20th century to do with philosophy of science, and in particular by people like

489
00:45:46,096 --> 00:45:51,524
Northwood Hanson and Thomas Kuhn and so on, who talked about theory-ladenness of observations.

490
00:45:52,256 --> 00:45:59,536
Because one of the key problems with the proposal that people like Robin Gandhi were making was the I was that he was making an assumption, I think.

491
00:46:00,174 --> 00:46:06,096
Maybe I'm besmirching him slightly, but he was making the assumption that we could make essentially a bare metal,

492
00:46:06,656 --> 00:46:12,416
like an unbiased observation of bare metal reality, that we could build a mathematical model of a physical system.

493
00:46:12,590 --> 00:46:19,136
we could perform observations of that system at the bare metal level, and we could just verify or falsify whether the model, you know, match the predic,

494
00:46:19,216 --> 00:46:21,136
whether the predictions actually match physical reality.

495
00:46:21,742 --> 00:46:26,596
But as Hansen and Kuhn and others correctly pointed out, that's really not how observation works.

496
00:46:27,086 --> 00:46:33,572
That anytime you make an observation, anytime you perform a measurement, you are doing so relative to an existing theoretical framework.

497
00:46:33,760 --> 00:46:36,324
And that's true both semantically and perceptually.

498
00:46:36,654 --> 00:46:39,120
So semantically, when we say

499
00:46:40,174 --> 00:46:44,000
like we've detected a particle or something, okay, what do we actually mean? What we actually mean is,

500
00:46:45,120 --> 00:46:50,400
there was a visual qualium that was induced in the mind of the experimenter that was produced by some, you know,

501
00:46:50,480 --> 00:46:54,480
presumably some photons that came off a computer screen that stimulated some photosynthesis of cells.

502
00:46:54,670 --> 00:47:01,120
And then we, you know, if we keep going, we can trace that back to the supposition that there were some particles in a calorimeter that produced some Cherenkov

503
00:47:01,280 --> 00:47:03,428
radiation that got detected by a scintillator, et cetera.

504
00:47:03,758 --> 00:47:11,520
But there's like, when you trace it out, there's probably 20 to 50 levels of theoretical assumptions that go into that statement of I detected a particle.

505
00:47:11,726 --> 00:47:14,080
Well, you didn't detect the particle, you just saw some dots on a screen.

506
00:47:14,320 --> 00:47:23,812
The interpretation of those dots on a screen as a particle is dependent on a set of on a theoretical apparatus that you cannot directly test by that observation because you've had to presuppose it.

507
00:47:24,174 --> 00:47:27,140
So that that's a there's a notion of semantic theory-ladeness.

508
00:47:27,822 --> 00:47:34,320
But there's also a notion of perceptual theory-ladeness, which is that your sense perceptions themselves are determined by your model of the world.

509
00:47:34,480 --> 00:47:37,440
And the, you know, the classic example of that is an optical illusion, right?

510
00:47:37,600 --> 00:47:45,280
Depending on, if you see an Escher painting or something, the way that you actually perceive that observation, it's the same observation.

511
00:47:46,000 --> 00:47:53,600
you're receiving the same set of photons, but the way you actually perceive it, the way you make sense of it, depends on whether you think this object is in front of this one,

512
00:47:53,680 --> 00:48:02,240
or whether these people are going up or these people are coming down, or whether the object is rotating right or rotating left. And you can change your perception of the same stimulus or the

513
00:48:03,200 --> 00:48:05,764
same phenomenon by modifying your model of the world.

514
00:48:06,320 --> 00:48:06,916
And so

515
00:48:07,822 --> 00:48:16,080
if you want to try and automate the process of doing experimental verification and falsification, you are not doing so in a completely unbiased and

516
00:48:16,480 --> 00:48:26,000
bare metal way. You have to find a way of systematically doing so in a manner that effectively parameterizes over the set of theoretical apparatuses

517
00:48:26,240 --> 00:48:28,560
that you could have assumed in performing the measurement.

518
00:48:28,720 --> 00:48:35,520
And this is a level of like philosophical and technical complexity that you never have to wrestle with in math and you never have to wrestle with in theoretical physics.

519
00:48:35,600 --> 00:48:43,140
It is, it is, but it is something that I think is critical if we are going to get effectively like recursively improving capabilities in the natural sciences.

520
00:48:45,006 --> 00:48:50,500
What is it your lab is doing differently than, say, the large AI labs currently, like Anthropic and OpenAI?

521
00:48:51,310 --> 00:48:59,972
Yeah. So, you know, we we are obviously not either on a resource level or on a personnel level able to compete with them in terms of things like compute and post-training and things like that.

522
00:49:00,240 --> 00:49:06,560
So what we are effectively doing is, you know, we're focusing on the development of what I was describing before,

523
00:49:06,640 --> 00:49:13,284
of these kind of formalized reasoning languages for scientific discovery, for in particular for things like physical reasoning.

524
00:49:13,678 --> 00:49:20,960
In the first instance, focusing on the mathematical and theoretical aspects, but with an ongoing project, and we're in the process actually at the moment of hiring some people to work on this,

525
00:49:21,120 --> 00:49:22,480
of also folding into

526
00:49:23,280 --> 00:49:26,116
automated laboratories and experimental pipelines and these kinds of things.

527
00:49:26,560 --> 00:49:34,640
So we are not primarily in the business of developing new models, although we do a little bit of that too, just for you know, for kind of entertainment

528
00:49:34,800 --> 00:49:43,760
value. We are primarily in the business of developing new reasoning languages for models, analogous to lean and uh you know Agda and things for mathematics

529
00:49:43,920 --> 00:49:47,360
